\f4\fs26 After many centuries harvesting music from a mere 8 notes, musicians finally and simply ran out of ideas. Everything musical had been thought of before. Either that or musical talent fell away as the centuries passed. Contemporary \'93music\'94, for want of a better term, was just awful. Talking in the stead of singing, rhythm but no melody or was it melody with no rhythm \'96 either way, it was unrecognisable as music. It was better described as angry, rarely rhyming poetry set to shitty tunes ripped off some old TV show. Real music fans did not like it. They did not like it at all. \
The infamous geneticist and music devotee, Dr. Linus Grawling, despised the criminal wailings of allegedly talented musicians and decided action, radical action, must be taken. After years of questionable and mostly criminal research and after decades of scouring the globe, Grawling gathered together the DNA of all the iconic musicians, alive or dead, that he could reasonably lay his hands on. He raided mausoleums, stole from crypts, robbed graves and mantelpieces for ash-filled urns. He then began cloning and cloning and cloning. After the obligatory several hundred unspeakable failures and numerous run-ins with the law, he found one genetic code that would readily lend itself to petri-dish replication. He has stumbled across the DNA during his stint at the University of Memphis, while on the run from the PTD. It had belonged to a famous local singer of antiquity, a relic all the way from the mid-20th century, but records relating to this particular singer were not intact and it was, therefore, impossible to know exactly who he was other that than his first name, Elvis. \
The cloned Elvises, or Elvi as they were collectively known, were a huge hit. With just a little training they could sing and dance like no one else and if you kept them on a restrictive diet, they didn\'92t even get fat. Grawling had unearthed a rich vein of talented genes that could not be suppressed, however, the ease at which one could clone an Elvis was only matched by the difficulties met in controlling the powerful hard-wired urges every Elvis held within. They were all genius musicians and singers, but could not hold back their unyoked enthusiasm for popular memes, bizarre fashions and guns. For reasons Grawling could never uncover, they were especially susceptible to spiritual manipulation which he worked hard, over many years, to remove from the genome, but with limited success.\
To fulfill their potential as entertainers the Elvi had to be quarantined from the influence of spirituality and religion, but fortuitously, in the Primary Trade Dominion shielding the Elvi from religious icons and fallacious spiritual memes wasn\'92t not too difficult. Religion having been squeezed out of the market decades prior. Even so, Elvi ownership came with a responsibility to ensure your Elvis or Elvises remained untouched and ignorant all things godly or spiritual, unless you wanted to void the manufacturer\'92s warranty.\
These guys were meant to entertain and entertain they did. Every shitty bar, discotheque and nightclub had at least one Elvis, although most had many more and some had nothing but Elvis employees. They all had an overwhelming urge to show off wild karate moves while wearing bejeweled suits and flared trousers. They were consummate performers and would happily sing for supper and dance for dessert. 
